/*
# ChoiceControl

A choice among several, **drawn as you like**: the control holds what is chosen, and its
**direct children** that have a `choice` are the things chosen — each a `ChoiceItem`, a
tile, a chip, a row with a picture. It has no look of its own: its layout arranges the
items, and anything else among them (a heading, a divider) is part of the drawing.

One of several, the default, is a `value`; several at once, with `multiple = true`, is
`values`. Either binds with `<->` and is what a `Form` judges.

```declare
class Key extends ChoiceItem [ width = 72, height = 44, cornerRadius = 10,
    fill = { on ? 0x1E2A38 : 0xE7EBF1 },
    TextLabel [ x = center, y = center, text = { classroot.choice },
        textColor = { classroot.on ? 0xFFFFFF : 0x1E2A38 } ]
    ]

App [ width = 360, height = 140, fill = white,
    size: string = "M",
    tags: string[] = [],
    ChoiceControl [ x = 20, y = 20, value <-> app.size, accessibilityLabel = "Size",
        layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = x, spacing = 8 ],
        Key [ choice = "S" ], Key [ choice = "M" ], Key [ choice = "L" ]
        ],
    ChoiceControl [ x = 20, y = 76, multiple = true, values <-> app.tags, accessibilityLabel = "Extras",
        layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = x, spacing = 8 ],
        Key [ choice = "Ice" ], Key [ choice = "Milk" ], Key [ choice = "Shot" ]
        ]
    ]
```

The control is one Tab stop, landing on the chosen item (with several, the first chosen).
The arrows move between the items; with one of several they choose as they move, and
with several Space adds or removes the item they are on. A screen reader hears a list of
options, each said as selected or not, and "2 of 3".

`RadioGroup` is a `ChoiceControl` with the radio's look and words; reach for a
`ChoiceControl` when the look is your own.

## multiple
Whether several may be chosen at once — `values` is then the value — or one, as `value`.
Fixed for the control's life, as its role is.

## accessibilityMultiselectable
Follows `multiple`: a screen reader is told whether several may be chosen.

## value
The chosen item's `choice`, with one of several. Standalone the control owns it;
constrained, override `commit` beside it, or write `value <-> app.size`.

## values
The chosen items' `choice`s, with several at once, in the order they were chosen.

## commit()
Where the choice goes: `value`, or `values` with several. Override it to own the destination
(`commit(v: string) { app.size = v }`), or bind with `<->`; the items keep working
untouched, because they deliver rather than store.

## pick()
Chooses an item by its `choice`, as the person would, through `edit` and `commit`: with one
of several it becomes the value; with several it is added, if it is not already chosen.

## toggle()
With several at once, adds the item to `values` or takes it out — what an item's press
does. With one of several, the same as `pick`.

## has()
Whether an item's `choice` is chosen now — what `ChoiceItem.on` reads.

## fieldValue
`value`, or `values` with several: what `required` and `validate` judge (see
`ValueControl`). An empty `values` is empty, so `required` asks for at least one.

## focusScope
`stop`: the control is one Tab stop, and the arrows move between its items.

## focusEntry()
The item Tab lands on: the chosen one (with several, the first chosen), else the first
that is enabled.

## _arrowed()
**Internal.** With one of several, the control chooses the item an arrow lands on.

## _items()
**Internal.** The control's items, for the Tab entry; a handler-only walk.
*/

/*
# ChoiceItem

One of the things a `ChoiceControl` chooses between: a direct child of the control, naming
itself with `choice`, and drawn as you like. It stores nothing — `on` is derived from the
control — and a press delivers its `choice` to the control. A `Radio` is a `ChoiceItem`
with a dot and a label.

## choice
This item's identity within its control, compared against the control's `value` (or found
in its `values`) by string. **Required and unique per control** — two items sharing a
`choice` are chosen together.

## on
Whether this item is chosen now. **Derived from the control, never stored** — which is why
choosing one can never leave another stale. Draw from it.

## press()
Delivers this item's `choice` to the control: chooses it, or with several adds or removes
it. Called by a click and by Space/Enter.

## onBlur
Judges the control when the focus leaves an item, as a field is judged on leaving. An override
calls `super.onBlur()`.

## accessibilitySelected
Follows `on`: whether this item is chosen.
*/

include [ "valuecontrol.declare" ]

class ChoiceControl extends ValueControl [
    accessibilityRole = listbox,
    accessibilityMultiselectable = { multiple },
    multiple: boolean = false,
    value: string = "",
    values: string[] = [],
    fieldValue = { multiple ? values : value },

    // the items are the stops, not the control (a ValueControl is otherwise one);
    // it is judged when the focus leaves its items

    focusable = false,
    focusField() { const r = focusEntry(); if (r != null) Focus.focus(r) },

    commit(v: any) { if (multiple) values = v as string[]; else value = v as string },
    has(c: string) -> boolean { return multiple ? ((shown ?? []) as string[]).includes(c) : shown == c },
    pick(c: string) { if (!multiple) edit(c); else if (!has(c)) edit([...((shown ?? []) as string[]), c]) },
    toggle(c: string) {
        if (!multiple) pick(c)
        else edit(has(c) ? ((shown ?? []) as string[]).filter((x) => x != c) : [...((shown ?? []) as string[]), c])
        },

    // one Tab stop, landing on the chosen item; with one of several the arrows
    // move and choose (planes.md §13.6), with several they move and Space chooses

    focusScope = stop,
    _arrowed(to: View) { if (!multiple && (to as any).choice !== undefined) pick((to as any).choice) },
    focusEntry() -> View? {
        const rs = this._items()
        return (rs.find((r) => has((r as ChoiceItem).choice)) ?? rs.find((r) => !(r as ChoiceItem).disabled) ?? null) as View | null
        },
    _items() -> array {
        const out = []
        for (const c of this.children) { if ((c as ChoiceItem).choice !== undefined) out.push(c) }
        return out
        }
    ]


class ChoiceItem extends Control [
    choice: string = "",
    accessibilityRole = option,
    accessibilitySelected = { on },

    // an item's parent is its control (the direct-child discipline above); the
    // cast informs the typecheck and is stripped for the runtime. `on` reads the
    // control's facts rather than calling has(), so an item misplaced outside one
    // is simply not on (the compiler says where)

    on: boolean = { (parent as ChoiceControl).multiple ? (((parent as ChoiceControl).shown ?? []) as string[]).includes(choice) : (parent as ChoiceControl).shown == choice },
    press() { (parent as ChoiceControl).toggle(choice) },

    // leaving the control's items is leaving the control

    onBlur() {
        super.onBlur()
        const g = parent as any
        if (g != null) g.judged = true
        }
    ]
